lessons.body:
HTTP Security Headers and CSP
TaskFlow - Full Stack Developer 2026

Fastify security headers
pnpm --filter api add @fastify/helmet@13.1.1

// main.ts - register before routes are exposed
const fastify = app.getHttpAdapter().getInstance()
await fastify.register(helmet, {
global: true,
contentSecurityPolicy: false, // API returns JSON; web CSP belongs to Next.js edge
})

For the JSON API, headers such as nosniff and transport-related protections are still useful, while a page-level
CSP is primarily a concern of the Next.js document responses.

Web header baseline
// next.config.ts - illustrative baseline
async headers() {
return [{
source: "/(.*)",
headers: [
{ key: "X-Content-Type-Options", value: "nosniff" },
{ key: "Referrer-Policy", value: "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" },
{ key: "Permissions-Policy", value: "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()" },
{ key: "X-Frame-Options", value: "DENY" },
],
}]
}

CSP rule
Do not paste an over-broad CSP containing unsafe-inline/unsafe-eval just to make warnings disappear. Build
and test the policy against actual Next.js scripts, Supabase endpoints and assets; prefer nonce/hash-based
policies when the deployment requires inline bootstrap code.

